"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,529\nThe Learning Brain. Series 5: Belonging\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,391\nEpisode 5\n\n3\n00:00:07,471 --> 00:00:09,629\nValues affirmation\n\n4\n00:00:12,429 --> 00:00:15,807\nCan a brief writing exercise narrow an achievement gap?\n\n5\n00:00:16,298 --> 00:00:20,586\nIn 2006, a study published in Science said yes,\n\n6\n00:00:20,822 --> 00:00:22,682\nand education took notice.\n\n7\n00:00:22,852 --> 00:00:25,698\nThe exercise is called values affirmation.\n\n8\n00:00:26,135 --> 00:00:28,585\nStudents choose values that matter to them,\n\n9\n00:00:28,703 --> 00:00:32,685\nlike family, friends, music,\n\n10\n00:00:32,705 --> 00:00:35,247\nor sports, and write briefly about why.\n\n11\n00:00:35,564 --> 00:00:41,561\nThe theory: reminding students of a larger sense of self buffers the threat of being judged through a stereotype.\n\n12\n00:00:41,731 --> 00:00:43,101\nIn the original study,\n\n13\n00:00:43,335 --> 00:00:46,852\nseventh graders did the exercise early in the school year.\n\n14\n00:00:47,379 --> 00:00:49,320\nFor African American students,\n\n15\n00:00:49,559 --> 00:00:54,013\nit reduced the racial achievement gap in the course by about forty percent,\n\n16\n00:00:54,373 --> 00:00:57,796\nand follow ups reported benefits lasting two years.\n\n17\n00:00:57,966 --> 00:00:59,579\nThen came bigger tests.\n\n18\n00:00:59,895 --> 00:01:02,932\nA district wide study reported a smaller benefit,\n\n19\n00:01:03,176 --> 00:01:04,482\nabout a quarter of the gap.\n\n20\n00:01:04,966 --> 00:01:09,322\nBut a large, careful replication in the same district found no effect,\n\n21\n00:01:09,506 --> 00:01:12,783\nand could rule out benefits larger than about point one.\n\n22\n00:01:13,354 --> 00:01:15,848\nOther replications also came up empty.\n\n23\n00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:20,110\nAnd a college biology study found an effect in one semester,\n\n24\n00:01:20,275 --> 00:01:21,431\nbut not the next.\n\n25\n00:01:21,601 --> 00:01:24,987\nSo values affirmation is not a reliable fix.\n\n26\n00:01:25,329 --> 00:01:27,064\nIt may help in some settings,\n\n27\n00:01:27,073 --> 00:01:31,407\nperhaps where threat is high and the exercise is delivered with care.\n\n28\n00:01:31,861 --> 00:01:33,912\nBut no one can yet predict where.\n\n29\n00:01:34,082 --> 00:01:34,861\nFor teaching.\n\n30\n00:01:35,211 --> 00:01:35,939\nIf you try it,\n\n31\n00:01:35,959 --> 00:01:37,441\ntreat it as an experiment.\n\n32\n00:01:37,834 --> 00:01:39,316\nKeep it brief, private,\n\n33\n00:01:39,336 --> 00:01:43,351\nand ungraded, early in the term or before a high stakes test.\n\n34\n00:01:43,749 --> 00:01:44,904\nTrack what happens.\n\n35\n00:01:45,174 --> 00:01:48,760\nAnd don't let it replace the structural work in the rest of this series.\n\n36\n00:01:49,131 --> 00:01:52,326\nOne tip to share with students: before a big test,\n\n37\n00:01:52,479 --> 00:01:54,987\ntake a minute to write down what matters to you.\n\n38\n00:01:55,157 --> 00:01:57,222\nPromising ideas deserve testing,\n\n39\n00:01:57,304 --> 00:01:58,229\nnot faith.\n\n"
